---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout2026.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Story"
  description="Twenty-six years of shipping interfaces — the long way, told properly."
  bodyClass="page page--story"
>
  <!-- TOC — sticky chapter rail (desktop only via CSS) -->
  <aside class="toc eyebrow" aria-label="Story chapters">
    <h2 class="toc__label eyebrow">Chapters</h2>
    <ol class="toc__list">
      <li><a href="#origin"><span class="toc__num">01</span><span>Origin</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#airline"><span class="toc__num">02</span><span>The Airline</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#lab"><span class="toc__num">03</span><span>The Lab</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#pet-empire"><span class="toc__num">04</span><span>The Pet Empire</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#two-million"><span class="toc__num">05</span><span>Two Million Users</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#whats-next"><span class="toc__num">06</span><span>What's Next</span></a></li>
    </ol>
  </aside>

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="meta hero__meta">
      <span class="meta__section m1">[ 02 ] The Story</span>
      <span class="meta__section m2">Long-form / 26-year arc</span>
      <span class="meta__section m3">~12 min read &centerdot; 6 chapters</span>
      <span class="meta__section m4">v.2026.05</span>
    </div>
    <div class="hero__main">
      <div class="hero__content">
        <div class="eyebrow-rule hero__eyebrow">
          <span>Twenty-six years, six chapters</span>
          <span class="rule eyebrow-rule__rule"></span>
          <span>Audience-aware</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="hero__headline"><span class="u-solo">The long</span> way <em>round</em></h1>
        <p class="hero__sub">
          From <em>hand-writing XSLT</em> for a flight-booking page at nineteen, to leading the team behind a developer tool
          with <em>two million daily users</em>. Not a résumé &mdash; a story about how the people you build with shape
          the things you ship.
        </p>
        <div class="audience-strip eyebrow" data-audience-strip>
          <span class="audience-strip__label">I'm here as a&nbsp;&nbsp;&rarr;</span>
          <div class="audience" role="tablist" aria-label="Audience">
            <button class="audience__btn is-active" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-aud="all">
              Reader
            </button>
            <button class="audience__btn" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-aud="hiring">
              Hiring manager
            </button>
            <button class="audience__btn" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-aud="founder">
              Founder
            </button>
            <button class="audience__btn" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-aud="peer"> Peer </button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTERS — inserted below -->

  <!-- CHAPTER 01 · Origin -->
  <section class="chapter" id="origin" aria-labelledby="ch-origin-title">
    <aside class="chapter__rail">
      <span class="chapter__num"><em>01</em></span>
      <span class="chapter__years sub">&mdash; 2003</span>
      <span class="chapter__tag eyebrow">Beginnings</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="chapter__body">
      <h2 class="chapter__title" id="ch-origin-title">Origin.</h2>
      <p class="chapter__lede">Building pages before there was a degree to want one.</p>
      <div class="chapter__prose">
        <p>
          <span class="chapter__lift">[Draft]</span> I'm a self-taught designer-engineer who grew up with the early web &mdash;
          the medium where the entire stack was visible if you knew how to <em>View Source</em>. By twenty I'd built
          more sites than I could remember the URLs to: landing pages, e-commerce mock-ups, a few logos for friends'
          bands, a Geocities-era fan site I still won't link to.
        </p>
        <p>
          There was no university for the job I wanted, so I built one in my browser tabs. I learned Photoshop, then
          HTML, then CSS, then JavaScript &mdash; in that order, because that's the order the work arrived in. The path
          from &ldquo;design this&rdquo; to &ldquo;ship it&rdquo; was one person long, and that person was me.
        </p>
        <p>
          That order matters. Twenty-three years later I still think the most useful UI engineers are the ones who
          learned to design and ship in the same gesture.
        </p>
      </div>
      <blockquote class="chapter__pull">
        <p>I learned to <em>design</em> and <em>ship</em> in the same gesture.</p>
        <cite>// chapter 01</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <figure class="chapter__figure">
        <div class="image-slot eyebrow">[Image: early portfolio screenshot or a photo from ~2002]</div>
        <figcaption>Fig. 01.01 &mdash; Replace with a screenshot of your earliest still-extant work.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 02 · The Airline -->
  <section class="chapter" id="airline" aria-labelledby="ch-airline-title">
    <aside class="chapter__rail">
      <span class="chapter__num"><em>02</em></span>
      <span class="chapter__years sub">2011</span>
      <span class="chapter__tag eyebrow">Spirit Airlines</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="chapter__body">
      <h2 class="chapter__title" id="ch-airline-title">The <em>Airline.</em></h2>
      <p class="chapter__lede">Modernizing a million-dollar booking flow at twenty-five.</p>
      <div class="chapter__prose">
        <p>
          <span class="chapter__lift">[Draft]</span> I walked into Spirit Airlines' offices in Fort Lauderdale and inherited
          a website running a million ticket sales a year on nested HTML tables, inline styles, and a tangle of XSLT no one
          had touched in years. The brief was nominally &ldquo;make it look better.&rdquo; The reality was a structural rebuild
          done while the engine was running.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nine months later it shipped on a modular CSS architecture, a reusable JS pattern library, and a set of
          conventions for the next team to keep building with.
          <span class="chapter__lift"
            >Same revenue. Faster pages. A codebase the next engineer could actually open.</span
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          The lesson was about leverage. Legacy isn't a curse &mdash; it's a deadline, and the team that respects the
          deadline gets to write the next chapter.
        </p>
      </div>
      <blockquote class="chapter__pull">
        <p>Nine months. One website. <em>A million</em> tickets.</p>
        <cite>// chapter 02 · spirit airlines · 2011</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <figure class="chapter__figure chapter__figure--wide">
        <div class="image-slot eyebrow">[Image: Spirit Airlines booking flow &mdash; before / after]</div>
        <figcaption>Fig. 02.01 &mdash; Replace with a before/after of the booking flow.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 03 · The Lab -->
  <section class="chapter" id="lab" aria-labelledby="ch-lab-title">
    <aside class="chapter__rail">
      <span class="chapter__num"><em>03</em></span>
      <span class="chapter__years sub">2013 &mdash; 2019</span>
      <span class="chapter__tag eyebrow">ion interactive</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="chapter__body">
      <h2 class="chapter__title" id="ch-lab-title">The <em>Lab.</em></h2>
      <p class="chapter__lede">
        Twelve years inventing tools at a marketing-tech company you probably haven't heard of.
      </p>
      <div class="chapter__prose">
        <p>
          <span class="chapter__lift">[Draft]</span> ion interactive (later ScribbleLive) made the platform that let non-engineers
          build interactive marketing content. For six years I made the things that let other people make things &mdash;
          the highest-leverage UI job there is.
        </p>
        <p>
          A first-to-market in-browser grid builder that snapped responsive layouts together with draggable tiles. A
          design system that survived four product redesigns. A participant-survey engine that turned quiz logic into a
          visual pipeline. The lab taught me that the UI which matters most is usually the UI that other people will
          build on top of.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also taught me to love a team. Twelve years is enough time to ship through three economic cycles, two
          acquisitions, and one global pandemic &mdash; with mostly the same people. That part doesn't go on a résumé.
          It goes here.
        </p>
      </div>
      <blockquote class="chapter__pull">
        <p>The UI that <em>matters most</em> is usually the UI other people build <em>on top of.</em></p>
        <cite>// chapter 03 · ion interactive · 2013–19</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <figure class="chapter__figure">
        <div class="image-slot eyebrow">[Image: ion content builder UI · grid editor]</div>
        <figcaption>
          Fig. 03.01 &mdash; Replace with a screenshot of the ion content builder or design system.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 04 · The Pet Empire -->
  <section class="chapter" id="pet-empire" aria-labelledby="ch-pet-title">
    <aside class="chapter__rail">
      <span class="chapter__num"><em>04</em></span>
      <span class="chapter__years sub">2020 &mdash; 2022</span>
      <span class="chapter__tag eyebrow">Chewy · Chirp</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="chapter__body">
      <h2 class="chapter__title" id="ch-pet-title">The Pet <em>Empire.</em></h2>
      <p class="chapter__lede">Shipping Chirp inside one of e-commerce's biggest brands.</p>
      <div class="chapter__prose">
        <p>
          <span class="chapter__lift">[Draft]</span> At Chewy I joined Chirp, the design system, as a manager and individual
          contributor &mdash; the rare arrangement that lets you shape the bet and ship the work in the same week. The bet
          was simple:
          <span class="chapter__lift">
            treat Chirp like a SaaS for an internal customer (every engineer at Chewy), and the system would defend
            itself across reorgs.
          </span>
        </p>
        <p>
          That meant docs that didn't lie. Releases with changelogs. Office hours. A roadmap visible to the whole org.
          An on-call rotation. Versioned tokens. A real Storybook. Real types. Real tests. All the infrastructure of a
          product, applied to a thing that had previously been treated as a style guide.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three reorgs later, Chirp is still standing. It's powering web, iOS, and Android. The engineers who used to
          fight design-system adoption now defend the system from the outside. That's the bet paying off.
        </p>
      </div>
      <blockquote class="chapter__pull">
        <p>Three reorgs later &mdash; <em>still standing.</em></p>
        <cite>// chapter 04 · chewy · chirp design system</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <figure class="chapter__figure">
        <div class="image-slot eyebrow">[Image: Chirp Storybook or design system docs]</div>
        <figcaption>Fig. 04.01 &mdash; Replace with a Storybook or component library shot.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 05 · Two Million Users -->
  <section class="chapter" id="two-million" aria-labelledby="ch-two-million-title">
    <aside class="chapter__rail">
      <span class="chapter__num"><em>05</em></span>
      <span class="chapter__years sub">2022 &rarr;</span>
      <span class="chapter__tag eyebrow">GitKraken · GitLens</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="chapter__body">
      <h2 class="chapter__title" id="ch-two-million-title">Two Million<br /><em>Users.</em></h2>
      <p class="chapter__lede">GitLens, an extension, and what open-source maintenance teaches you about leadership.</p>
      <div class="chapter__prose">
        <p>
          <span class="chapter__lift">[Draft]</span> GitLens is the largest Git extension on the Visual Studio Marketplace
          &mdash; twenty-nine million installs, two million daily active users. I joined as a maintainer, which is the closest
          thing modern software has to a parish job: people you've never met, with problems you didn't cause, expecting answers
          in your repo at midnight.
        </p>
        <p>
          Open source taught me what twenty years of internal product work couldn't: that the hardest decisions involve
          people you don't share a Slack with. That deprecating an API isn't a release-notes event, it's a relationship.
          That every line of public code is a promise you have to be willing to keep &mdash; or to break, kindly, on the
          record.
        </p>
        <p>
          The work itself is the same shape as ever: shipping interfaces that real humans rely on. The scale is
          different. Two million daily users teaches you to write the diff comment you'd want to read.
        </p>
      </div>
      <blockquote class="chapter__pull">
        <p>Every line of public code is a <em>promise</em> you have to be willing to keep.</p>
        <cite>// chapter 05 · gitlens · 2022–</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <figure class="chapter__figure chapter__figure--wide">
        <div class="image-slot eyebrow">[Image: GitLens UI · Inspect view or commit graph]</div>
        <figcaption>Fig. 05.01 &mdash; Replace with a current GitLens screenshot.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 06 · What's Next -->
  <section class="chapter" id="whats-next" aria-labelledby="ch-next-title">
    <aside class="chapter__rail">
      <span class="chapter__num"><em>06</em></span>
      <span class="chapter__years sub">Now &rarr;</span>
      <span class="chapter__tag eyebrow">Looking forward</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="chapter__body">
      <h2 class="chapter__title" id="ch-next-title">What's <em>next.</em></h2>
      <p class="chapter__lede">
        Building for the way teams actually work &mdash; not the way org charts pretend they work.
      </p>
      <div class="chapter__prose">
        <p>
          <span class="chapter__lift">[Draft]</span> Twenty-three years in, the work I want next sits at three intersections:
          <em>engineering leadership where design and engineering are one conversation</em>,
          <em>developer-facing surfaces treated as customer surfaces</em>, and
          <em>tools that ship at the speed of the people using them</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          That means front-end-heavy product organizations where the design system, the documentation, and the CLI all
          matter. It means leadership roles where I get to keep my hands in the code while making the team smarter than
          any one engineer on it. It means staying close to open source.
        </p>
        <p>
          If that's the shape of the team you're building &mdash; for senior engineering, design-systems leadership,
          developer-experience product work, or just a long conversation about how the work should feel &mdash;
          <a href="/#connect">let's talk</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- END / CTA -->
  <section class="section section--end">
    <h2 class="section__headline">Thanks<br />for <em>reading.</em></h2>
    <nav class="nav-bigass section__actions">
      <a class="nav-bigass__link" href="/work">See the work</a>
      <a class="nav-bigass__link" href="/#connect">Get in touch</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
